<onlyinclude>'''Silicon''' (chemical symbol '''Si''', atomic number 14) is a group-14 chemical element and the principal crystalline substrate used in conventional silicon microelectronics. Its diamond-cubic lattice and indirect electronic band gap support semiconductor devices whose conductivity can be controlled by substitutional dopants, electrostatic fields and temperature. Silicon is not synonymous with a finished semiconductor device: fabrication adds patterned insulating, conducting and doped structures.</onlyinclude>

== Structure and electronic properties ==
Crystalline silicon has a diamond-cubic structure with a room-temperature lattice parameter of approximately 0.5431 nm. Its indirect band gap is approximately 1.12 eV at 300 K; these are temperature- and material-condition-dependent reference values, not universal constants.<ref name="byu">[https://www.cleanroom.byu.edu/Semiconductor-Constants Brigham Young University Cleanroom, ''Semiconductor Constants'', silicon property table].</ref>

== Doping and applications ==
Group-III acceptors such as boron commonly produce p-type silicon; group-V donors such as phosphorus commonly produce n-type silicon. Doping modifies carrier populations but does not change silicon's elemental identity. Single-crystal wafers underpin integrated circuits and photovoltaic cells. Polycrystalline and amorphous forms have distinct structural and electronic properties.

== Silicon dioxide interface ==
Controlled thermal oxidation produces silicon dioxide, important historically for passivation, isolation and gate dielectric structures. The classical Deal–Grove growth model describes diffusion/reaction-limited regimes but is not generally reliable for the first few nanometres of oxide.<ref name="dg">B. E. Deal and A. S. Grove, [https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1713945 "General Relationship for the Thermal Oxidation of Silicon"], ''Journal of Applied Physics'' 36, 3770–3778 (1965).</ref><ref name="thin">[https://doi.org/10.1155/2016/7545632 "A Self-Consistent Model for Thermal Oxidation of Silicon at Low Oxide Thickness"], 2016.</ref>

